    "narrative": "Today\u2019s offensive engine is anchored by **James Wood** (78.42) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75), who stand as our strongest plays against a favorable matchup with Paredes. **James Wood** benefits from a high-upside combination of a hitter-friendly park and a generous 4.76 xERA matchup, while **Mickey Moniak** capitalizes on a dynamic platoon advantage and a premium spot in the two-hole. **Josh Naylor** (67.55) also grades out as a core play; his production is bolstered by an elite matchup against Cecconi and a high-leverage spot in the middle of the order. In our quest for scarcity, keep a close eye on **Jose Caballero** (62.42) and **Corbin Carroll** (61.58); both carry significant utility, with **Jose Caballero** providing a high-ceiling boost thanks to a strong platoon split and an environment ripe for stolen base opportunities.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Tyler Soderstrom**, who landed just outside our starting criteria with a 46.29 score. Despite a solid base projection, he was squeezed out by the sheer volume of high-scoring outfielders and infielders, and his matchup against the 3.32 xERA of Detmers posed too much of a hurdle for a marginal start. We are also monitoring the matchup for **Cole Young** (46.67), whose score is suppressed by a high-efficiency catcher behind the plate, limiting his ability to exploit the stolen base environment we typically chase. While **Brice Turang** holds a 55.33, he remains a volatile start against Peterson, D, who possesses an elite hold rate that significantly caps the stolen base potential we crave. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the risk factors inherent in today's slate. **Seiya Suzuki** (50.57) faces a challenging afternoon against Harrison, and while his order placement saves his efficiency score, the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill level remains a meaningful drag on his production. Similarly, **Henry Bolte** (47.84) is a direct play for his spot at the top of the order, but he faces a tough 3.32 xERA hurdle. Ensure all lineup positions are confirmed before first pitch, particularly for those players batting lower in the order, as any late-game adjustments could further erode the marginal value provided by **Dalton Rushing** (40.75) or **Zach McKinstry** (47.14).",
